<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maribor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marburg an der Drau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maribor is the second largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-styria">Lower Styria</a>. A medieval market town of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> with a largely German-speaking population, it was secured for the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> in 1918–1919 by Rudolf Maister's forces. It is a centre of wine-growing, claims the world's oldest living grapevine, and was a European Capital of Culture in 2012.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maribor.jpg" alt="Maribor" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vlada Republike Slovenije · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mariborski_Glavni_trg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
